(D)  ITEM 3 -- B2 / B3 NULLS.  Owed three rounds; computed here or struck.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   B2 answer = count at n=20 ; B3 answer = count at n=22.  The NULL model is stated
   before the numbers: a non-computing judge sees only n and 'at least two parts'.
   The honest prior is the TREND over neighbouring n, which the judge can also see
   nothing of -- so the null is measured as: over the answer band actually occupied
   by n in 12..26, what fraction of integers in that band is the true answer?
      n=12   B2-type: 11   of 76    terminating   B3-type: 25  
      n=14   B2-type: 13   of 134   terminating   B3-type: 35  
      n=16   B2-type: 15   of 230   terminating   B3-type: 47  
      n=18   B2-type: 17   of 384   terminating   B3-type: 60  
      n=20   B2-type: 19   of 626   terminating   B3-type: 74  
      n=22   B2-type: 21   of 1001  terminating   B3-type: 90  
      n=24   B2-type: 23   of 1574  terminating   B3-type: 107 
      n=26   B2-type: 25   of 2435  terminating   B3-type: 125 

   B2 (n=20) TRUE ANSWER = 19   over 626 terminating partitions with >=2 parts
   B3 (n=22) TRUE ANSWER = 90   over 1001 terminating partitions with >=2 parts
   occupied band across n=12..26 : B2 [11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 21, 23, 25] (width 15)  B3 [25, 35, 47, 60, 74, 90, 107, 125] (width 101)
   uniform-in-band null : B2 = 1/15 = 6.7%   B3 = 1/101 = 1.0%
   MONOTONE-TREND null, the stronger adversary: the counts are monotone in n over
   this range, so a judge who computed ONE neighbouring n and extrapolated does
   better than uniform.  That is a REASON TO STRIKE B2/B3 FROM A FUTURE BRIEF, not
   a reason to cite them.  RECORDED AS: computed, low-value, and the r31 declines
   at both seats stay worth exactly zero -- as r33 already recorded.
   B2/B3 nulls are computed, not owed                               OK  (B2=19, B3=90)

